  <HOMEWORK title="Homework 15: Same jitter spec, different frequency, different result"
            prompt="This link passed its jitter tolerance check comfortably at low offset frequency. For a compliance sweep, someone re-ran the same nominal sinusoidal jitter amplitude at a much higher offset frequency, and now the receiver can't hold a clean lock. Nobody touched the receiver's clock recovery settings between the two runs. What's actually different?"
            hint="Measure the jitter actually hitting the receiver with the Jitter / Phase Noise Scope - read off the dominant tone's frequency. Then check the receiver's CDR loop gains (Kp/Ki) in its editor - are they unusually low, or just normal?"
            explanation="The CDR's Kp/Ki are left at their normal defaults - this isn't a misconfigured receiver (contrast that with the earlier &quot;CDR too slow&quot; homework, where the gains themselves were the problem). The sinusoidal jitter here is simply at a much higher offset frequency than before, with the same amplitude - and every clock recovery loop has a limited tracking bandwidth no matter how well-tuned its gains are. Jitter well below that bandwidth gets tracked (the CDR's recovered phase just follows it, so bits still land in the right slot); the same amplitude far above it cannot be tracked, so the phase error eats directly into the slicer's margin instead. This is exactly the idea behind a real jitter-tolerance mask in SerDes compliance specs: tolerable sinusoidal jitter amplitude is large at low offset frequency and drops off sharply at high offset frequency, tracing out the receiver's own CDR bandwidth. Passing a jitter tolerance check once, at one frequency, doesn't prove it passes across the whole spec - you have to sweep frequency, not just amplitude, and the Jitter / Phase Noise Scope is how you confirm what frequency you actually stressed it at."/>
</LABCHAIN>
